1. Ne2 { In this section, the focus will be on identifying and targeting weak squares. The examples will help you to spot key weak squares and how to target them, as well as the link between weak squares and other topics like piece play, tactics, and calculation. A recurring theme in your games will be the d3 and d6 squares, as these squares are central and are often left undefended as the central pawns push forward in the early opening. We saw an example of this the previous game, with Black landing a queen and then a knight on the d3 square. In this case, it is Black who will leave the d6 square as a target. } e5 $2 { Black wants to equalise the situation in the centre, but in so doing they leave themselves with a weak square on d6. } 2. { -KEY- } Nc4 $1 { A key problem that Black faces is that their dark-squared bishop, the ideal defender of the d6 square, has already been fianchettoed. } Ke7 $6 { Black covers d6 in a risky way, hoping that the absence of queens will stop White from punishing the uncastled king. } ( 2... Bf8 $6 { may have been the lesser evil, but after a simple developing move like } 3. Be3 $16 { White stands much better due to the greater development, while after 0-0-0 next, they will be once again threatening to jump into d6. } ) 
 ( 2... Nb6 { may have been best, but after } 3. Nd6 Ke7 4. Nxc8 $16 { White has a stable advantage with the bishop pair. } ) 
3. b3 { Preparing Ba3+ next. } f6 4. Ba3+ c5 $2 { Black makes a second mistake. Advancing the pawn takes the sting away from Nc4-d6 ideas, but now there is an outpost for White to exploit on d5. } ( { Stepping the king back with } 4... Kd8 { was the lesser evil, but for a human this is psychologically difficult to play since White can follow up } 5. O-O-O { when the Black king will be in the line of fire. } ) 
5. O-O-O ( { Moves like } 5. Rd1 { or } 5. Ne3 { are similar in strength. } ) 
b6 6. Kc2 $1 ( { Rushing with } 6. Ne3 { would be a mistake due to the annoying pin after } Bh6 $13 ) 
Nh6 7. Ne3 Bb7 8. Ng3 Rhd8 9. Bc4 Rac8 10. Bc1 Ng8 11. a4 $16 { White has every part of the board under control. They can jump to d5 with the knight at the right moment, or perhaps first build up an edge on the kingside with h4-h5. } 
