 { Here, the d6 square is not only weak but is in fact an outpost. Can you find a two-move sequence for White to target it? } 2. { -KEY- } Bxc4 $1 { Happily giving up the bishop pair, to gain access not just to the b5 square but also to e4. } dxc4 { With the Black pawn driven away from d5, White gains access to the e4 square. This may be useful in the near future. } 3. Ndb5 $1 { Just one mistake from Black, and they find themselves in a near-losing position. White will continue with moves like Nc3-e4 and Qd1-g4 next. Notice that not only is White targeting the weak d6 square, but also creating a broader strategy against Black's compromised dark squares. } ( 3. Ncb5 $6 { would be much less strong, since this knight should be used to target d6 via the e4 square.Depending on the openings you play, you will find opportunities to attack the d6 square more or less often. If you're a 1.e4 player, you should watch out for these possibilities in popular openings like the French Defense or in some variations of the Sicilian Defense. While less common, you will also encounter other weak central squares like e3 or e6. Let's show an example of this next. } ) 

