 { In chess, one mistake will often dictate the nature of the play over the next series of moves. This 'chain effect' is unsurprising, since the chess board is interconnected. In the position below, we can see how the initial weak square on e3 leads to a devastating chaining effect. } 8... { -KEY- } Ng4 $1 { Black immediately threatens to occupy the e3 square with a fork against White's heavy pieces, so a quick reaction is needed. } ( 8... b6 $5 { was played in the game Alan Thomas,G - Alekhine,A (1925). The former World Champion's move was a very natural one, but in playing it he missed an opportunity to immediately capitalize on the central weak square in White's position. } ) 
9. Nb3 { White clears the path of the c1 bishop in order to cover the square. This is the only move that doesn't immediately lose. } ( { Sidestepping the fork with } 9. Qe1 $2 { is not possible, since after } Ne3 $19 { Black is winning material. } ) 
dxe4 $1 { The chain effect begins. The White knight is no longer covering the e4 pawn, so now the capture on e4 forces the exchange of queens. } 10. dxe4 Qxd1 $1 11. Bxd1 ( { Forced, since } 11. Rxd1 Nf2 { would allow Black to win an exchange. However, the consequence of this move is that now the bishop is no longer on the f1-a6 diagonal. Black can capitalise on this with his next move. } ) 
c4 $1 12. Nc5 { White has no choice but to drive the knight into the Black position, although here it will feel quite uncomfortable since Black can at any moment play ...b6. } ( { If } 12. Nbd2 { then either } c3 $1 { or } Ne3 { offer Black a large advantage. } ) 
c3 $1 { Black creates new positional problems! } 13. b3 $6 { A human would likely play this move, seeking to keep the position as closed as possible. However, Black's advanced pawn is a major thorn in White's side. } ( { The computer suggests } 13. e5 { as the lesser evil. However, after } cxb2 14. Bxb2 Ne3 $17 { Black has already fractured White's pawn structure, and is about to gain the bishop pair on the next move. } ) 
Rd8 $1 { Black's initiative continues, seizing the open d-file and threatening to win a piece. } 14. Kg1 { Stopping the tactical idea. } b6 $1 { Gaining time and pushing the knight back to a passive square. } 15. Na4 { The saying 'a knight on the rim is dim' certainly holds true here, but the alternative was even worse. } ( 15. Nd3 $2 { runs into tactical problems after } Rxd3 $1 16. cxd3 c2 $19 { with a winning position for Black. } ) 
Nf6 $1 { Black targets the undefended e4 pawn. } 16. e5 ( { If } 16. Re1 $2 { Black can still take the pawn. } Nxe4 $1 17. Rxe4 Rxd1 $19 ) 
Nd5 $17 { Black is close to winning here, as he can continue to develop the initiative over the coming moves, while White's piece coordination is hard to remedy.In your own games, it's a good idea to check if you can use the chain effect to your advantage when you spot a defect in the opponent's position.In the next few examples, we'll discuss the link between targeting weak squares and piece play. } 
