 { One of the things that makes evaluating weaknesses tricky is that it's not enough to rely on the structure, you must also consider other elements in the position. The most important of these is your piece placement relative to the weak squares. Let's take a look at two near-identical positions. The first one, below, is approximately equal. This might be surprising since structurally Black's queenside is in some danger. The a7 pawn is undefended, while the c6 and a6 squares are weak squares. So why is it that Black has no problems? The answer has to do with Black's strong light-squared bishop which is able to play a defensive role and in this way compensate for the structural weaknesses. } 1. { -KEY- } Nb5 { If White tries to invade, Black is ready to eliminate the knight. } Bxb5 $1 2. cxb5 { An eye for an eye! White now enjoys an outpost on c6, but on the other hand, the d5 square has become an outpost for Black. Importantly, White now has an isolated queen's pawn on d4. Such pawns are fragile as they require constant piece protection. These elements lead to a dynamically balanced middlegame. } Na5 $1 { Black begins by addressing the threat of Ne5-c6. } 3. Qd3 { Connecting the rooks and overprotecting the a3 pawn. White is also now better prepared to slot the dark-squared bishop back to d2 to challenge Black's knight on a5. } ( { Challenging the knight would be desirable, but it drops a pawn after } 3. Bd2 $2 Qxd4 $17 { . } ) 
Nd7 $1 { Black is in time to exchange White's central knight. } 4. Rac1 Nxe5 5. Bxe5 Qd7 $11 { Black has a solid position, with their better structure offsetting the bishop pair. How would Black have fared if they had been missing the critical light-squared bishop in the starting position? Let's take a look in the following example. } 
