 { This position arose in an online game played between Magnus Carlsen and GM Sergei Zhigalko. Can you find the powerful 2-move sequence that Magnus missed? } 1. { -KEY- } Nb5 $1 { Black is unable to exchange off the knight, leaving them with no good reply against the pressure to the a7 pawn. } a6 { Black tries to save the pawn by advancing it. } ( 1... Ra8 $2 { This passive move runs into an immediate tactical refutation. } 2. Nxa7 $1 { With this move, White wins a clean pawn. } Rxa7 3. Nc6 { Black has to give up either their bishop or their rook. } Qa8 4. Nxe7 Kh8 5. Nc6 { White's maneuvering has netted them a pawn, while if Black tries to recover it with: } Rxa3 6. Rxa3 Qxa3 7. Qb1 $1 { It turns out that Black has no way to save the weak b6 pawn. For example: } Qa6 8. Qb4 Re8 9. Rb1 { The pawn will fall soon, since the attempt to hang on to it with } Nd7 { is met by } 10. c5 $18 { when ...bxc5 would lose to Qxb7. } ) 
2. Na7 $1 { Well done if you spotted this unusual idea in advance! By embedding the knight deep in enemy territory, White not only attacks the rook but also targets the weak square on c6. } Ra8 ( { Forced, any other square loses the exchange (e.g. } 19... Rc7 20. Nec6 $1 { and 21.Bxc7). } ) 
3. Nac6 { The knight lands on the juicy c6 square with a tempo on the Black queen. } Qe8 4. Rb1 $1 { Once again we see the chaining effect in action. Black's pawn on b6 is now very difficult to protect after Black was forced to play ...a6. } Bd8 { Black tries to defend the pawn,. } 5. Nxd8 $1 Qxd8 6. Nc6 $1 { In this way White forces the win of the b6 pawn. } Qd7 7. Rxb6 $18 { These two examples have shown us that it is not enough to consider the structure. Instead, it's essential to consider the harmony between the structure and piece placement. For now we'll close out this topic, but we'll be revisiting it later in the course when we come to the chapter on 'Piece Play'. The final section of the chapter will address a mix of important topics that don't fit neatly into one bucket, beginning with endgame play. } 
