 { The influence of the remaining pieces on the board can have a great effect even in simplified positions. Can you spot the key weakness in the Black position, and find the right first move for White? } 1. { -KEY- } Kc3 $1 { Correct! White targets the weak square on c5. This is one of the major problems of having isolated pawns. } ( 1. Ke3 $2 { In the game, White chose the wrong square, enabling Black to put a stop to the advance of the king. } c5 $1 2. Kd2 { White had to step back, but the loss of two tempi gave Black the time needed to mount a successful defense. } Rbf8 3. Kc3 f5 $1 { Black generates counterplay of their own, with a well-timed pawn break. } 4. Kc4 g5 $1 5. Rxf5 Rxf5 6. exf5 Rxf5 $11 { Black is fine, because White cannot go into the king and pawn endgame due to Black's dangerous 2 vs 1 kingside pawn majority. In fact, if White errs here with } 7. Rxf5 $2 { then after } Kxf5 8. Kxc5 h5 $1 { Black wins the pawn race and with it the game. One mistake was all it took for the advantage to evaporate. } ) 
Rbf8 { Black adopts the same strategy as in the Ke3 variation above, but here they lack the time needed to pull it off. } 2. Kc4 f5 { Black tries to get rid of their f-pawn, as the need to constantly defend it is zapping all of their resources. } ( 2... Kd6 { Black cannot hold on by covering the c5 square with their king. } 3. b4 $1 f5 4. a5 Rf6 5. c3 R6f7 6. exf5 $1 { A well-timed capture, with White's idea being to invade with the rooks along the central files. } Rxf5 7. Rd4 $1 Ke6 8. Re1 Kf6 9. Rd7 Rf7 10. Rd8 $18 { The isolated pawn on a7 will soon fall, and with its loss Black's position becomes hopeless. } ) 
3. Kc5 Kd7 { Unfortunately for Black, their plan has failed and they must now go passive. What's worse is that their f-pawn is now pinned and must continue to be monitored. } ( 3... fxe4 $2 { The following is more the domain of calculation than it is positional play. But for any curious student, I have left the full unannotated queening sequence to show that Black cannot play in this way. } 4. Rxf7 $1 Rxf7 5. Rxf7 Kxf7 6. dxe4 $18 g5 7. Kxc6 h5 8. Kxc7 Ke6 9. Kb7 g4 10. c4 h4 11. c5 g3 12. hxg3 hxg3 13. c6 g2 14. c7 g1=Q $11 15. c8=Q { with a winning queen and pawn endgame for White. } ) 
4. d4 $18 { Eventually, White will convert this endgame. Their plan, depending on what Black does, is likely to involve advancing the b-, c- and e-pawns up the board and overwhelming Black with a central pawn steamroller. We saw the importance of precise calculation in some of the variations within this example. Contrary to some people's belief, strong positional players are usually very solid tacticians. This is because positional ideas often have to be backed up by precise calculation, as we'll see in the next couple of examples. } 
