 { Yasser Seirawan, in his book 'Winning Chess Strategies' writes that 'In chess, a pawn or square is only weak if it can be attacked.' While it is often the case that a weakness will be impossible to attack, there are times when the target is 'hidden in plain sight'. In such moments, tactical vision is the key to implementing your strategic desires. Can you see what White's best move would be in the following position? } 1. { -KEY- } Nb5 $1 { A bolt from the blue! White exploits the pinned c-pawn to enter into enemy territory with their knight and take aim at the weak d6 square. } O-O { Black castles in order to defuse the threat of 2.Nc7+, winning material. However, White has a second strong possibility available to them, which was missed in the game Gurevich,D - Zhou,J (2005). } 2. Bd6 $1 { An extremely strong idea, supported by the fact that Black cannot trade bishops without losing material. } ( 2. Nd6 $6 { In the game, White forced the win of the bishop pair. However, after: } Bxd6 3. Bxd6 Re8 4. Be2 Ne4 $1 5. Bf4 Nf8 $15 { Black's centralised knights were more than a match for White's bishop pair, with the resulting middlegame being approximately equal. } ) 
Qd8 { The only move that offers chances of survival. } ( 2... Bxd6 $2 3. Nxd6 $18 { wins a piece. } ) 
 ( 2... Re8 $2 { also loses a piece. } 3. Bxe7 Rxe7 4. Nd6 $18 { White will take on f5 next. } ) 
3. Qa3 $1 { Black can no longer avoid material losses. } Bxd6 { Accepting the loss of a pawn is the lesser evil. } ( 3... Re8 $2 { can be met by } 4. Bxe7 Rxe7 5. Nd6 { with a similar outcome to our main line. } ) 
4. Nxd6 Bg6 5. Nxb7 $1 { White wins a clean pawn and leaves Black with a weak backward pawn on c6 to boot. In our final game of this section, we will turn to a game played by Magnus Carlsen where the tactical and calculation demands of the position made exploiting the weak squares in the enemy camp a very tricky task! } 
