 { In an online game, Magnus Carlsen is up against the strong Armenian GM Aram Hakobyan. Can you guess the surprisingly strong move that Magnus found in the game? } 1. { -KEY- } Nh4 $3 { Completely ignoring the pressure against the centre and instead immediately targeting the weak f5 square. While technically not a weak square, Black cannot play ...e6 without allowing their e7-d6 pawn chain to collapse. Therefore, it functions very similarly to a weak square. } dxe5 { In the game, Black saw nothing better than to grab the 'free' central pawn. } ( 1... Bxe2 { Other approaches do not save Black. Here's one sample line: } 2. Qxe2 dxe5 3. Nf5 $1 { White occupies the weak square and immediately threatens to win Black's bishop on g7. } Bf6 4. d6 $1 { Opening up further lines of attack against the Black position. } exd6 5. Nxd6 Kf8 6. Qxh5 { Threatening mate on f7. } Rh7 7. fxe5 Nxe5 8. Nce4 $18 { White's pieces are swarming Black's uncoordinated forces, exploiting the many weak squares in Black's half of the board. } ) 
2. Bxg4 hxg4 3. Nf5 $1 { Occupying the weak square and threatening to capture the bishop on g7. } Bf6 4. Qxg4 $1 { Black's extra pawn is meaningless since White's superior forces leave the pieces of the enemy uncoordinated and his king in constant danger. } ( 4. d6 $6 { was Carlsen's choice in the game, but it loses a chunk of the advantage as it has the drawback of opening up the e6 square for Black's knight. } ) 
e6 5. dxe6 Nxe6 6. Ne4 $1 { White can continue in several ways, and with the best play, it is only a matter of time before the attack crashes through. Some key takeaways from this section: 1. The most important weaknesses tend to be squares at or near the centre, and preferably advanced points. The most common of these are the d3/d6 squares, arising from various popular openings. 2. The relationship between weak squares and piece placement can spell the difference between manageable weaknesses and game-ending weaknesses. In particular, be careful not to part ways with the same color bishop as the squares you are weakest on. 3. The chain effect is extremely important in chess, and one weakness can snowball into a whole range of further defects. 4. At the same time, do not be paranoid about creating weaknesses. They are inevitable in pursuit of your overall strategy. 5. Oftentimes, you will need to rely on your tactical and calculation abilities to make full use of weak squares in the enemy position. In my view, it is impossible to be a strong positional player without being proficient in the more dynamic areas of the game. In the next section, we'll discuss two more topics: defending weak squares, and how to create them. } 
 *
